WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.188 --> 00:00:04.553
AlphaNIST: Emergent Assembly
Skills from Binary Task Rewards.

2
00:00:04.961 --> 00:00:08.436
The NIST board is an assembly
benchmark with twenty tasks,

3
00:00:08.436 --> 00:00:12.369
including gear meshing, threading,
and connector insertion.

4
00:00:12.766 --> 00:00:16.179
We use binary task rewards, cached resets,

5
00:00:16.179 --> 00:00:19.343
beta sampling, and large-scale
reinforcement learning.

6
00:00:19.752 --> 00:00:23.502
The task reward arrives only
when assembly is complete.

7
00:00:23.502 --> 00:00:28.125
We do not prescribe grasping,
reorientation, or insertion stages.

8
00:00:28.546 --> 00:00:33.296
Training starts from a bank of loose
parts, held parts, and partial assemblies.

9
00:00:33.296 --> 00:00:36.249
These are starting states,
not demonstrations.

10
00:00:36.673 --> 00:00:41.723
Beta sampling puts more training effort
into states the policy sometimes solves,

11
00:00:41.723 --> 00:00:44.391
while retaining a chance
to revisit every state.

12
00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:49.483
We train with up to two hundred and
fifty-six thousand parallel environments.

13
00:00:49.911 --> 00:00:55.479
One policy learns all twenty individual
tasks, using point clouds and robot state.

14
00:00:55.878 --> 00:00:59.315
Training across assemblies
gives higher training success

15
00:00:59.315 --> 00:01:01.437
than learning each task separately.

16
00:01:01.907 --> 00:01:05.934
Success increases as we train
with more parallel environments.

17
00:01:06.288 --> 00:01:09.901
Adding dense rewards for reaching
and alignment lowers success

18
00:01:09.901 --> 00:01:12.932
by twenty percentage points
at the largest scale.

19
00:01:13.272 --> 00:01:17.310
Replacing beta sampling
with uniform sampling lowers success

20
00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:21.046
by thirteen percentage points,
compared with the full method.

21
00:01:21.448 --> 00:01:23.686
The gripper holds the gear at an angle,

22
00:01:23.686 --> 00:01:26.856
turns it into alignment,
and lowers it onto the post.

23
00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:31.547
This grasp leaves too little
of the peg exposed.

24
00:01:31.547 --> 00:01:34.506
The robot sets it down
and grasps nearer the end.

25
00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:38.341
It moves this peg away from
the board edge before picking it up.

26
00:01:39.708 --> 00:01:44.062
The gripper presses the connector against
the board to turn it before insertion.

27
00:01:44.756 --> 00:01:47.631
Threading varies with nut size and reach.

28
00:01:47.631 --> 00:01:52.294
On the left, a fingertip taps
the small, nearby M4 nut.

29
00:01:52.294 --> 00:01:56.481
In the middle, the outside
of a finger turns the farther M12 nut.

30
00:01:56.481 --> 00:01:59.539
On the right, the gripper
circles the nearby M8 nut.

31
00:01:59.949 --> 00:02:01.949
For multi-component tasks,

32
00:02:01.949 --> 00:02:05.936
the reward arrives only when
all active parts are assembled.

33
00:02:05.936 --> 00:02:09.849
The robot moves parts aside
and separates overlapping gears

34
00:02:09.849 --> 00:02:12.293
before returning
to the remaining assemblies.

35
00:02:17.035 --> 00:02:18.860
With a neighboring gear in place,

36
00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:23.329
the gripper rotates the raised gear
to align the teeth, then lets it settle.

37
00:02:23.713 --> 00:02:26.738
Beside an inserted
Ethernet connector, the gripper

38
00:02:26.738 --> 00:02:30.879
makes short turns and repositions
its fingers to keep its hand clear.

39
00:02:31.276 --> 00:02:36.888
Binary task rewards and training at scale
can produce dexterity and coordination.

40
00:02:36.888 --> 00:02:40.863
Shared training improves training success
over separate specialists;

41
00:02:40.863 --> 00:02:43.726
adding dense shaping lowers success.

42
00:02:43.726 --> 00:02:47.667
Multi-part policies also learn
which parts to work on and when.
